NAME
    chd - convert the library's disc games to CHD, check them, list them

SYNOPSIS
    chd ls [<path>...]
    chd convert <path>... [--fast] [-j <threads>] [--output <folder> | --beside]
    chd check [<path>...]
    chd wait [chd<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    The CHD tool of the emusen.library module. A <path> is a file or a
    folder of the library, AppSettings.RomDirectory, written relative to
    it with / between its parts: psx, "psx/Rayman (USA)", SCD. / is the
    whole library, and so is no path for ls and check. The disc games
    under each path are its PlayStation and Sega CD discs: a cue sheet
    and its tracks, a lone raw track, an ISO image, or a zip holding one.
    Which console a file is for is the library scan's own routing, then
    the nearest console folder above it (psx, segacd).

    THE LIBRARY IS ONLY READ, unless a conversion is given --beside.
    Nothing under it is written, renamed or removed: no CHD, no package,
    no link. A batch whose output folders and the library overlap is
    refused before anything is written. With --beside the one thing
    written into the library is each disc's new CHD (see BESIDE THE
    ORIGINAL); no original is ever written, renamed or removed.

    'convert' converts each disc with chdman, MAME's CHD manager, which a
    build ships beside the platform library. The CHD goes to the output
    folder, at the disc's path in the library with .chd (psx/Rayman
    (USA).cue becomes <output>/psx/Rayman (USA).chd), first as a partial
    file that is renamed into place only when three checks pass in the
    same run:

        1  chdman's own verify: every hunk decompressed, the overall SHA-1
           compared;
        2  every track rebuilt from the CHD through EmuSen's own reader,
           a reader other than chdman's, and its CRC-32, MD5 and SHA-1
           compared with those taken of the original before chdman ran;
        3  the table of contents (tracks, their pauses and starts, the
           lead-out and the disc type) equal to the original disc's.

    Checks 2 and 3 are not run over a CHD that check 1 found damaged; the
    disc has failed in any case. Each converted disc is given a linked
    package in Packages/<console>/, whose package.json names the CHD and
    records the original dump: every track's hashes, the sheet's, the
    form, and the CHD's codecs and SHA-1s.

    A disc already converted, whose package names a CHD still at the size
    and time recorded and whose originals have not changed, is skipped,
    so that a stopped batch run again converts only the rest. A disc is
    started only with the free space of its original and 64 MiB more at
    the output; one whose original changes during its conversion is
    discarded; a zip of several tracks with no sheet, a sheet naming a
    WAVE file, and a file that is neither a raw track nor an ISO image are
    skipped with the reason. One disc failing never stops a batch.

    THE OUTPUT FOLDER is Compressed unless AppSettings.ChdOutputDirectory
    names another, in appsettings.json or from the Compressed Discs
    window; --output <folder> names one for this command alone, a path of
    the shell (/ is EmuSen's home), so a folder outside the home is named
    in the setting. The folder follows the rules of every folder setting:
    ~ is the home, a relative path or a file is refused, and one not yet
    there is made. It may be neither inside the library nor hold it;
    either is refused with words before anything is written. A disc's
    package names a CHD outside Compressed by its full path, so changing
    the setting later leaves every earlier CHD found where it was, and a
    disc already converted anywhere is not converted again.

    --fast writes chdman's Zstandard codecs, cdzs,cdfl, in place of its
    defaults, cdlz,cdzl,cdfl: about 1.3 to 1.8 times faster to decode for
    a CHD about a point larger. -j sets the threads chdman compresses
    with; all of the machine's by default.

    'check' runs the three checks again on discs already converted,
    against their packages, wherever their CHDs are; the originals are
    not read. 'ls' lists the disc games with their state: converted, not
    converted, changed (an original changed since its conversion) or
    missing (its CHD is gone); a CHD beside its original is marked so.

BESIDE THE ORIGINAL
    --beside writes each disc's CHD into the library, in the folder of
    its original, as <stem>.chd: psx/Rayman (USA)/Rayman (USA).cue gets
    psx/Rayman (USA)/Rayman (USA).chd. It is a choice for the one command
    and never a setting, so that no script and no forgotten setting
    writes the library. Before the first disc, the command's first line
    says that it writes into the library and how many CHD files it adds
    at most: the discs not converted whose name is free.

    The originals are never removed, renamed or overwritten. Where a
    file of the CHD's name is already there, that disc is left as it is
    with the reason, and nothing is written for it. chdman writes to
    <stem>.chd.partial-<process> in the same folder; the partial is
    renamed to <stem>.chd only after the three checks pass, by a rename
    that refuses to replace a file (renameat2's RENAME_NOREPLACE on
    Linux), and is removed when any check fails or the job is stopped. A
    partial a killed process left is removed an hour later, the next
    time that disc is converted: only a file of that exact name. Should
    the package not be written, the CHD just placed is taken out again,
    so the library never holds a CHD without its record.

    The library shows such a game once, launching the CHD, while the
    tool's record verifies it. The record is the disc's package, not the
    file's name: its container is a CHD whose game is in the library,
    whose three checks are each recorded as passed with its SHA-1, whose
    CHD has the size and time recorded, and whose every original file
    still has the size and time it had. Then the package claims the
    originals, and the scan lists the package alone. Without the record
    (no package, or one that does not verify) the CHD and the original
    are both listed, each opening as itself, and a convert leaves the
    disc as it is, its name being taken. The originals stay on disk in
    every case.

JOBS
    Each command runs as a job, followed one line a disc until it ends or
    the line's budget is spent. A job that outlives the budget goes on in
    the background as chd<n>: 'ps' lists it with how far it has got,
    'chd wait chd<n>' follows it again, and 'kill chd<n>' stops it, which
    ends chdman and removes its partial file, so that nothing of the
    current disc is left. Every disc's outcome is also written, one line
    each, to Logs/chd_<day>.log.

CHDMAN
    The chdman run is the one pinned in Platform/library-tool/chdman.pin:
    MAME 0.289, built from MAME's source archive by build-chdman.sh. A
    chdman whose SHA-256 is not the pinned one is refused with both
    hashes named, and nothing is converted. chdman's source file is under
    the BSD 3-Clause License, copyright Aaron Giles, and MAME as a whole
    under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or later;
    THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md lists its licences and the written offer of
    its source.

EXAMPLES
    chd ls psx
    chd convert psx SCD
    chd convert "psx/Crash Bandicoot (USA).bin" --fast
    chd convert psx --output /Compressed-PS1
    chd convert SCD --beside
    chd check psx
    ps
    chd wait chd1
    kill chd1

